      "text": "From the given information, we can write the following equations:\n\n1. $x = \\frac{1}{2}y$ (The value of $x$ is one-half the value of $y$)\n2. $y = \\frac{1}{5}z$ (The value of $y$ is one-fifth the value of $z$)\n3. $z = 60$ (We are given that the value of $z$ is 60)\n\nFirst, we substitute the value of $z$ into the second equation to find the value of $y$:\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{1}{5} \\cdot 60 = \\frac{60}{5} = 12\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute the value of $y$ into the first equation to find the value of $x$:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 12 = \\frac{12}{2} = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of $x$ is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).\n\nThe quantity of new innovative products launched is normally modeled by a Poisson random variable. Suppose that the mean number of new products launched per year is 30. Find the probability that more than 10 products will be launched next year.\n\nTo find the probability that more than 10 new products will be launched next year, we start by noting that the number of new products launched each year follows a Poisson distribution with a mean \\(\\lambda = 30\\). The probability mass function of a Poisson random variable \\(X\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nP(X = k) = \\frac{e^{-\\lambda} \\lambda^k}{k!}\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(P(X > 10)\\). This can be calculated as:\n\n\\[\nP(X > 10) = 1 - P(X \\leq 10)\n\\]\n\nSo, we need to compute \\(P(X \\leq 10)\\), which is the sum of the probabilities from \\(X = 0\\) to \\(X = 10\\):\n\n\\[\nP(X \\leq 10) = \\sum_{k=0}^{10} \\frac{e^{-30} 30^k}{k!}\n\\]\n\nCalculating each term individually and summing them up:\n\n\\[\nP(X = 0) = \\frac{e^{-30} 30^0}{0!} = e^{-30}\n\\]\n\\[\nP(X = 1) = \\frac{e^{-30} 30^1}{1!} = 30e^{-30}\n\\]\n\\[\nP(X = 2) = \\frac{e^{-30} 30^2}{2!} = 450e^{-30}\n\\]\n\\[\nP(X = 3) = \\frac{e^{-30} 30^3}{3!} = 4050e^{-30}\n\\]\n\\[\nP(X = 4) = \\frac{e^{-30} 30^4}{4!} = 27375e^{-30}\n\\]\n\\[\nP(X = 5) = \\frac{e^{-30} 30^5}{5!} = 16807.5e^{-30}\n\\]\n\\[\nP(X = 6) = \\frac{e^{-30} 30^6}{6!} = 102000e^{-30}\n\\]\n\\[\nP(X = 7) = \\frac{e^{-30} 30^7}{7!} = 54656.25e^{-30}\n\\]\n\\[\nP(X = 8) = \\frac{e^{-30} 30^8}{8!} = 283500e^{-30}\n\\]\n\\[\nP(X = 9) = \\frac{e^{-30} 30^9}{9!} = 135135e^{-30}\n\\]\n\\[\nP(X = 10) = \\frac{e^{-30} 30^{10}}{10!} = 60480e^{-30}\n\\]\n\nSumming these probabilities:\n\n\\[\nP(X \\leq 10) = e^{-30} (1 + 30 + 450 + 4050 + 27375 + 16807.5 + 102000 + 54656.25 + 283500 + 135135 + 60480)\n\\]\n\\[\nP(X \\leq 10) = e^{-30} \\times 538172\n\\]\n\nUsing the value \\(e^{-30} \\approx 1.4214504492235652 \\times 10^{-12}\\):\n\n\\[\nP(X \\leq 10) \\approx 1.4214504492235652 \\times 10^{-12} \\times 538172 \\approx 7.587782573202907 \\times 10^{-7}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that more than 10 new products will be launched next year is:\n\n\\[\nP(X > 10) = 1 - P(X \\leq 10) \\approx 1 - 7.587782573202907 \\times 10^{-7} \\approx 0.9999999242629709\n\\]\n\nSo, the final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{0.9999999242629709}\n\\]\n\nMr. John J. Hickman, 81, a family man who moved to Humacao after his wife Nancy took him to Puerto Rico in 1971, died Thursday surrounded by family members at his Humacao home. Hickman was also employed as a mail carrier in Humacao.\n\nAnswer this question based on the passage: where is john hickman deceased?\n\nThe passage states that Mr. John J. Hickman, 81, passed away surrounded by family members at his Humacao home.\n\nStep 1: Identify the relevant information from the passage that answers the question. In this case, the answer is \"Humacao home.\"\n\nStep 2: Conclude the answer based on the identified information. Since the question asks for John Hickman's death location, the answer is \"Humacao home.\"\n\nThe final answer is: John Hickman was deceased at his Humacao home.\nYou are an AI assistant. User will you give you a task. Your goal is to complete the task as faithfully as you can. While performing the task think step-by-step and justify your steps.",
